{"schema":"plectis_maths_universe_excerpts_v2","paper":"erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface","excerpts":[["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:middle-allright-defect","The tail inequality at a final middle\ntransition","<p><em> Suppose row <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>13</mn></math></span> is middle and all rows after it\nare right. Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>D</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span> and\nput </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mrow><mi>rem</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></math></span>. Since <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo></math></span>, an estimate in the\nopposite direction would exclude this scenario. No such reverse estimate\nis assumed or proved here. Remark <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#rem:tail-dominance-open\">10.8</a> states the\ncorresponding sufficient hypothesis with the value <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></math></span> excepted.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:two-sided-dyadic","A conditional two-sided dyadic bound","<p><em> Assume the following two conditions for every row <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>s</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></math></span>: </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mtable displaystyle=\"true\" columnalign=\"right left\" columnspacing=\"0em\" rowspacing=\"3pt\" data-break-align=\"bottom top\" minlabelspacing=\"0em\"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></mrow><mtext> at </mtext><mi>s</mi></mtd><mtd><mstyle indentshift=\"2em\"><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟹</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mn>4</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mrow><mi>rem</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>∉</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>,</mo></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mrow><mtext> at </mtext><mi>s</mi><mtext> and </mtext><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>s</mi></msup></mtd><mtd><mstyle indentshift=\"2em\"><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟹</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Then at every row <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>s</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></math></span>, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>rem</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>s</mi></msup><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mtext>or</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> The first hypothesis concerns only middle\ntransitions. The second concerns only right transitions at which the old\novershoot is at most <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>s</mi></msup></math></span>. Neither\ncondition is asserted here for the whole sequence.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:upper-reset-band","Upper-reset dyadic-band escape, checked\nfor <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>13</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>30</mn></math></span>","<p><em> The\nupper-reset dyadic-band escape condition requires the following at every\nactual upper reset <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>13</mn></math></span>: for\nevery <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></math></span>, the reset charge\navoids a linear-width band immediately below the dyadic power <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></span> (<span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>resetCharge</mi></mrow></math></span> or\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>resetCharge</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></span>). Granted this, <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝒜</mi></mrow></math></span>. The linked proof verifies this\ncondition for <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>13</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>30</mn></math></span>, using\nexact successor remainders at rows <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>14</mn></math></span>–<span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>31</mn></math></span> (for example, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>rem</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>14</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>392</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>rem</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>31</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>4187487147</mn></math></span>). This finite\nverification does not supply the hypothesis for every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>13</mn></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:mobius-centred-nonneg","Möbius-centred carry nonnegativity below\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>","<p><em> If <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>∉</mo><mi>A</mi></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>, then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> for every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>N</mi></math></span>. Indeed, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>ihc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.</mn></math></span></div>\n<p><em> The carry is an integer,\nso it is at least <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span>; subtracting\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span> gives the asserted nonnegativity\nof the centred carry. The strict inequality and integrality are both\nneeded in this argument. The statement is about the divisor counts <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></math></span>, not a new irrationality\ncriterion.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:sqrt-bound-route","A square-root bound on the greedy\ncarry","<p><em> For the greedy support <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></math></span>, the centred carry is\nnonnegative. If in addition </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mtext>for every </mtext><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>G</mi></math></span> has infinitely many skipped indices and\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>. The unresolved part is\nthe upper bound along this particular orbit. The carry identity explains\nits strength: a positive gap <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> would contribute a term of\norder <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>N</mi></msup></math></span>, which cannot satisfy a\nsquare-root bound. This is distinct from the reset-deviation hypothesis\nof Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#thm:upper-reset-band\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:upper-reset-band\">6.10</a>; no\nequivalence between the two hypotheses is asserted.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:frozen-margin","Equivalent sign and vanishing\nconditions","<p><em> For the prefix <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>∩</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span> define </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>J</mi></msup><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> This is a finite integer expression: it compares the\nnext <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>J</mi></math></span> divisor counts of the fixed\nprefix with its centred carry. Let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></math></span> denote the integer greedy remainder\nat depth <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></math></span>, with weights <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> for <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></math></span> and target <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></math></span>. The following conditions\neach imply <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝒜</mi></mrow></math></span>:</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li><p><em>For every skipped rank <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>.</em></p></li>\n<li><p><em>For every skipped rank <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></math></span> at which the real and integer\ngreedy words agree on <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>.</em></p></li>\n<li><p><em>For every skipped rank <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></math></span>.</em></p></li>\n</ol>\n<p><em>Conditions (i) and (ii) are equivalent. Condition (iii) implies\nthem; no converse or strict separation is established here. These\nhypotheses must hold at every indicated skipped rank, not merely through\na finite computed range.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:full-support-catalogue","Erdős–Borwein full-support\nirrationality, unconditional","<p><em> For every integer <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>, the sum <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></span> is irrational.\nThis is Erdős’s theorem <span class=\"citation\"\ndata-cites=\"erdos1948\"><a href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#ref-1\">[1]</a></span>; at base <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn></math></span> the sum is the Erdős–Borwein constant.\nThe result concerns full support, not all its infinite subsets.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:pairwise-coprime","Pairwise-coprime support irrationality,\nErdős 1968","<p><em> For every integer <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> and every infinite pairwise-coprime\nsupport <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></math></span> with\nsummable reciprocals, <span class=\"math inline\"><math><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></munder><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> is irrational.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:weighted-coeff-engine","Irrationality from divisible coefficient\nblocks","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> be an\ninteger and let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mo>:</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span> satisfy <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></math></span> for\nevery <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi></math></span>. Suppose that for every\ninteger <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> there are nonnegative\nintegers <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></math></span>, with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>K</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>L</mi></math></span>, such that </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mtable displaystyle=\"true\" columnalign=\"right left right left\" columnspacing=\"0em 2em 0em\" rowspacing=\"3pt\" data-break-align=\"bottom top bottom top\" minlabelspacing=\"0em\"><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mtd><mtd><mstyle indentshift=\"2em\"><mi></mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mstyle></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mstyle indentshift=\"2em\"><mi></mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></munderover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mtd><mtd><mstyle indentshift=\"2em\"><mi></mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mtd><mtd><mstyle indentshift=\"2em\"><mi></mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mstyle indentshift=\"2em\"><mtext>for some integer </mtext><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mtd><mtd><mstyle indentshift=\"2em\"><mi></mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>.</mo></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></math></span> is\nirrational.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:lcm-gap-engine","Irrationality from a gap beyond the\npreceding least common multiple","<p><em> For an integer base <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> and strictly increasing support\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>:</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>: if <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>lcm</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></math></span>,\nthen <span class=\"math inline\"><math><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></munderover><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> is\nirrational. The base may vary over the integers, but the denominators\nremain <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>. Applying the\nargument to another denominator sequence would require its own\ndivisibility and tail estimates.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:factorial-twopow-support","Factorial-support and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup></math></span>-support instances","<p><em> For every\ninteger <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>, both </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>!</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> are irrational. These are\ninstances of the preceding theorem. For the factorial support the\npreceding least common multiple is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>k</mi><mo>!</mo></math></span> when <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, so the gap is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>k</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>k</mi><mo>!</mo></math></span>. For the powers of two it is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></span>, so the gap is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></span>. Both tend to infinity. The\nconclusion concerns these two supports, not every infinite\nsupport.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:multiples-support","Multiples-support irrationality via\ndilation","<p><em> For integers <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>\nand <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em>\nis irrational. This is the full-support result (Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#thm:full-support-catalogue\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:full-support-catalogue\">6.14</a>) at the\ninteger base <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></math></span>; no new support\nargument is required.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:periodic-support","Periodic-support irrationality","<p><em>\nLet <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> be integers. Suppose <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></math></span> is nonempty\nand satisfies <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></math></span> if and only if\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></math></span> for every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>. Then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> is irrational.</em></p>\n<p><em>The indicator of <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>A</mi></math></span> is a\nnonzero purely periodic integer weight, so this follows from Luca and\nTachiya’s theorem stated above. The linked declaration supplies a\nseparate formal proof. At <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> the\nonly nonempty periodic support is full support; residue classes and\nunions of residue classes give the other immediate examples.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:eventually-periodic","Eventually-periodic support\nirrationality","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> and\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> be integers. An infinite\nsupport whose membership is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi></math></span>-periodic from some threshold <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></span> onward has irrational <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:residue-odd","Residue-class and odd-support\nirrationality","<p><em> For integers <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> and a residue <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mtable columnspacing=\"0em\" rowspacing=\"0.1em\"><mtr><mtd><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>n</mi><mo>≡</mo><mi>c</mi><mspace width=\"0.444em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>mod</mi><mspace width=\"0.333em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></munder><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></span> is irrational. Specializing <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>: <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mtext> odd</mtext></mrow></munder><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> is\nirrational for every <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>, the\ncase treated explicitly in <span class=\"citation\"\ndata-cites=\"lucatachiya2017\"><a href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#ref-3\">[3]</a></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:signed-periodic","A formal dichotomy for signed periodic\nweights","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> be integers, and let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>w</mi><mo>:</mo><msub><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span> be\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi></math></span>-periodic. Put </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> The cited formal argument gives the\ndichotomy that <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>x</mi></math></span> is irrational or\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span> for some integer\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>. It excludes the latter\nalternative if <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></math></span> has one sign\nthroughout and is nonzero at arbitrarily large indices. These sign\nassumptions concern <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></math></span>, not <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>w</mi></math></span>. As noted above, Luca and Tachiya’s\ntheorem already excludes the terminating alternative for every nonzero\nperiodic <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>w</mi></math></span>, without either sign\nrestriction. The zero weight gives <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>.</em></p>"]],"source":"erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.json?v=054a6001eb83"}
